  "id": "COMPOUND:19464 - PROTEIN:5790",
  "verbalization": "Dorsmanin D is implicated in the inhibition of HPAC through multiple mechanistic pathways, all of which converge on the enzyme 5alpha,6alpha-Epoxy(22e,24r)-ergosta-8(14),22-dien-3beta,7alpha-diol. This compound is a target of both Dehydrogenase/reductase SDR family member 9 and Lysine-specific demethylase 4D-like, which are both involved in the metabolic processing of the epoxy compound. The inhibition of HPAC, a key enzyme in the biosynthesis of steroid hormones, is likely to occur through the direct interaction of Dorsmanin D with these targets, leading to the disruption of the metabolic pathway that ultimately affects HPAC activity. The consistent presence of the epoxy compound as a common target across different pathways suggests a coordinated regulatory mechanism, reinforcing the hypothesis that Dorsmanin D exerts its effects by modulating the activity of enzymes involved in the biosynthesis of steroid hormones, thereby influencing HPAC function. The evidence supports a direct, mechanistic link between Dorsmanin D and HPAC, with the shared target of the epoxy compound serving as a critical node in the pathway.",
